    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-43045",
      "sequence_position": 25,
      "problem": "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=3$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $1$st and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
